  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is an axon reflex? (3 marks)', 'An axon reflex is a local nervous response in which an impulse set up in one branch of a sensory nerve fibre travels up to a branching point and then back down (antidromically) along the other branches of the same axon, without passing through the spinal cord or brain. It is therefore not a true reflex, as there is no reflex arc or synapse.
Example: when the skin is scratched or injured, the impulse spreads along the branches of the sensory nerve, which release substance P and CGRP, causing the surrounding arterioles to dilate - the red ''flare'' of Lewis''s triple response. Axon reflexes also explain some sweating and piloerection responses used in tests of autonomic nerve function.', null, '[{"point": "A local response in which an impulse in one branch of a sensory nerve fibre travels antidromically (backwards) down other branches of the same axon, without passing through the spinal cord/CNS", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Not a true reflex - there is no reflex arc or synapse", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Example: the flare of the triple response - injury/scratch causes branches to release substance P/CGRP, producing local vasodilatation and redness around the injury", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
