  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Write two differences between vitamins and hormones. Give one example of each. (3 marks)', '1. Source: vitamins are organic substances needed in small amounts that the body cannot make in sufficient quantity, so they must come from food (except vitamin D, made in skin, and vitamin K, made by gut bacteria). Hormones are made inside the body by endocrine glands.
2. Mode of action: vitamins act mainly as coenzymes or cofactors in metabolic reactions within cells; hormones are chemical messengers secreted into the blood and carried to distant target organs, where they regulate growth, metabolism and reproduction.
3. Disorders: vitamin problems are mainly deficiency diseases (scurvy, rickets); hormone problems arise from over- or under-secretion (diabetes, goitre, gigantism).
Examples: vitamin - vitamin C (ascorbic acid); hormone - insulin.', null, '[{"point": "Source: vitamins must be obtained from the diet (mostly not made by the body); hormones are made by endocrine glands in the body", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Action: vitamins act mainly as coenzymes/regulators of metabolism; hormones are chemical messengers carried in blood to target organs; deficiency vs. over- or under-secretion diseases", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Examples: vitamin C (or A, D, B1); hormone insulin (or thyroxine, adrenaline)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
