  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Briefly describe the blood groups of human beings. (3 marks)', 'Human blood groups are based on antigens on the surface of red blood cells. The two most important systems are:
ABO system (discovered by Landsteiner): group A has antigen A and anti-B antibodies in the plasma; group B has antigen B and anti-A; group AB has both antigens and no anti-A or anti-B; group O has no A or B antigens but both antibodies.
Rh system: people whose red cells carry the D antigen are Rh positive; those without it are Rh negative. Anti-D antibodies form only after exposure to Rh-positive blood, through transfusion or pregnancy.
Importance: blood must be cross-matched before transfusion to avoid clumping and haemolysis - O negative is the universal donor of red cells and AB positive the universal recipient. Rh incompatibility between an Rh-negative mother and Rh-positive baby can cause haemolytic disease of the newborn.', null, '[{"point": "ABO system (Landsteiner): groups A, B, AB and O according to A/B antigens on red cells; plasma has the opposite antibodies (A - anti-B, B - anti-A, O - both, AB - none)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Rh system: Rh positive (D antigen present) or Rh negative; anti-D forms after exposure (transfusion/pregnancy)", "marks": 0.75}, {"point": "Importance: matching for transfusion (O negative universal donor, AB positive universal recipient), Rh incompatibility in pregnancy", "marks": 0.75}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
