  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Name the bacterium that causes tetanus. Name the vaccine that prevents tetanus. (3 marks)', 'Tetanus is caused by Clostridium tetani, a Gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming bacillus found in soil, dust and animal dung. Its spores enter the body through wounds (rusty nails, burns, unclean delivery or cord care), germinate and produce the toxin tetanospasmin, which causes painful muscle stiffness and spasms, starting with lockjaw.
Tetanus is prevented by the tetanus toxoid vaccine (an inactivated toxin). In Bhutan it is given in infancy as part of the pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) vaccine, with boosters as Td in childhood and adulthood, and to pregnant women to prevent neonatal tetanus. After a dirty wound, a booster dose of tetanus toxoid (and anti-tetanus immunoglobulin if not immunised) is given.', null, '[{"point": "Clostridium tetani - a Gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming bacillus found in soil and animal dung; enters through wounds", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Its toxin (tetanospasmin) causes muscle stiffness and spasms - lockjaw", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Prevented by tetanus toxoid vaccine - given as DPT/pentavalent in infancy with boosters (Td) and to pregnant women (prevents neonatal tetanus); TT/anti-tetanus immunoglobulin after dirty wounds", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
