  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What are the five functions of the skeletal system in the body? (3 marks)', '1. Support - the skeleton forms a rigid framework that gives the body its shape and supports soft tissues.
2. Protection - bones protect delicate organs: the skull protects the brain, the rib cage the heart and lungs, and the vertebral column the spinal cord.
3. Movement - bones act as levers; muscles attached to them by tendons pull on them at joints to produce movement.
4. Blood cell formation (haemopoiesis) - red bone marrow in flat bones and the ends of long bones produces red cells, white cells and platelets.
5. Storage - bones store minerals, especially calcium and phosphate, releasing them into the blood when needed, and yellow marrow stores fat.', null, '[{"point": "Support - framework that gives shape to the body; Protection of organs (skull - brain, ribs - heart and lungs, vertebrae - spinal cord)", "marks": 1.2}, {"point": "Movement - bones act as levers for muscles at joints; Blood cell formation (haemopoiesis) in red bone marrow", "marks": 1.2}, {"point": "Storage of minerals (calcium, phosphate) and fat (yellow marrow)", "marks": 0.6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
